{"id":"n-20261011-oumc3j","category":"world","display_title":"UK Parties Clash Over Climate Priorities","display_title_he":"המפלגות בבריטניה חלוקות סביב סדרי העדיפויות האקלימיים","short_summary":"Britain's party conference season highlighted deep divisions over climate policy, as Conservatives challenged green building standards and the Greens shifted on nuclear power.","english_level":"C1","words":456,"reading_time":3,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"The Guardian","domain":"theguardian.com","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2026/oct/11/party-conference-season-uk-climate-action-tories-greens-labour","title":"‘We need less point scoring’: What party conference season taught us about UK climate action","published_at":"2026-10-11T06:00:18.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-11T06:00:18.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-11T13:10:22.042Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["Britain’s annual political party conference season has exposed sharp realignments and growing fractures over national climate policy. With the country confronting severe environmental challenges — ranging from polluted waterways and declining wildlife to escalating heatwaves — the major political factions have adopted contrasting stances that challenge long-standing assumptions about environmental governance.","At the Conservative gathering in Birmingham, leadership hopeful Kemi Badenoch positioned the elimination of the Future Homes Standard (FHS) at the core of her bid for prime minister. The UK requires approximately six million new homes, and the FHS mandates low-carbon features such as heat pumps, solar arrays, and high-performance insulation. Conservative figures claimed compliance would cost £30,000 per property, but experts noted that building-stage installations actually cost £5,000 to £8,000, yielding annual bill savings exceeding £1,300. Retrofitting existing properties later would cost over £20,000. Jan Rosenow, a professor of energy policy at Oxford University, warned that constructing fossil-fuel-reliant homes creates long-term liabilities for occupants and causes investor uncertainty. Simon McWhirter, chief executive of the UK Green Building Council, noted that the construction industry has spent years preparing for the standard, investing in skills and supply chains. Reversing course, he warned, risks delaying progress. Furthermore, the Conservatives proposed abolishing Natural England and the Environment Agency. Robbie MacPherson, former head of the parliamentary group on climate, observed that voters will not reward leaders who spurn economic growth and environmental protection.","Meanwhile, an unexpected policy shift unfolded at the Green Party conference in Bournemouth. Delegates resolved to abandon the party's historic opposition to nuclear power. Under the revised stance, Green parliamentarians may support new nuclear reactors, acknowledging their potential contribution to low-carbon electricity, although renewable energy remains their priority. However, the shift sparked sharp criticism from energy analysts. Paul Dorfman, chair of the Nuclear Consulting Group, warned that nuclear power involves twenty-plus-year construction times, repeated delays, and massive cost overruns, thereby diverting scarce capital away from faster options like energy storage, renewables, and grid upgrades.","Electoral tensions were equally evident during the Holborn and St Pancras by-election, where Labour's Sagal Abdi-Wali won while facing a rising Green vote share. Greenpeace UK political director Ami McCarthy stressed that climate concern is surging as extreme heat and droughts become regular threats. At Labour’s conference, Greater Manchester Mayor Andy Burnham offered limited remarks on nature, though he announced a publicly owned body to invest in the energy grid alongside the privatised National Grid. Labour also outlined a community fund to plant 500,000 trees across England. Nevertheless, campaigners remain focused on North Sea oil drilling decisions, specifically regarding the Rosebank and Jackdaw sites, which were postponed for the by-election. With an El Niño system threatening severe heatwaves, droughts, and stormy weather, Friends of the Earth campaigner Rosie Downes urged political leaders to translate words into urgent climate action."],"facts":[{"fact":"Kemi Badenoch made scrapping the Future Homes Standard a central plank of her bid to become prime minister at the Conservative party conference in Birmingham.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Installing low-carbon fittings like solar panels and heat pumps during construction costs an estimated £5,000 to £8,000, while retrofitting them later costs over £20,000.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The Conservative party proposals included abolishing the Environment Agency and Natural England.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The Green Party voted at its conference in Bournemouth to abandon its long-standing opposition to nuclear power.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"In the Holborn and St Pancras by-election, Labour candidate Sagal Abdi-Wali won while the Green Party increased its vote share.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"At the Labour conference, Andy Burnham announced a publicly owned body to invest in the energy grid and a fund to plant 500,000 trees across England.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Decisions regarding the Rosebank and Jackdaw oil drilling sites were reportedly postponed ahead of the by-election.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"annual","lemma":"annual","pos":"adj","he":"שנתי","name":false},{"text":"political","lemma":"political","pos":"adj","he":"פוליטי","name":false},{"text":"party","lemma":"party","pos":"noun","he":"מפלגה","name":false},{"text":"conference","lemma":"conference","pos":"noun","he":"ועידה / כנס","name":false},{"text":"season","lemma":"season","pos":"noun","he":"עונה","name":false},{"text":"exposed","lemma":"expose","pos":"verb","he":"חשף","name":false},{"text":"sharp","lemma"